    35	The house standard for what is worth tracking (quoted): "A question is a bet an analyst would hold a card for: a big matter, never trivia (a
    36	deal, product or project opens one only when its scale can change revenue, profit, cash or credit risk). Small matters open no question …
    37	wordings, deadlines, milestones and periods of one bet are one question." "Most of what an item says is noise … new information that changes
    38	an answer is never missed."
    39	
    40	Mark:
    41	1. `worth`: "Is this a question worth tracking for this company?" Answer worth, trivia, or duplicate (duplicate only when an earlier-opened
    42	   question in the same library listed on the card is the same bet: the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move apart).
    43	2. `answer` (only when the card shows a latest answer): "Is the latest answer supported by the material it was given?" Answer supported,
    44	   unsupported (the material points to another answer, or the answer contradicts its reasons or material), or insufficient (the material is
    45	   too thin to choose any answer). Judge whether the reasons and the material justify the chosen answer over its neighbours.
    46	3. `why`: one or two sentences naming what decides your marks.
